# 6. WORKSTREAM D — MESH, TIME/LOAD-STEP AND NUMERICAL SENSITIVITY

Create reproducible sensitivity campaigns that vary one numerical factor at a time.

## D1. Mesh sensitivity

Use at least three systematically refined meshes with documented:

- Element type.
- Node and element counts.
- Mesh-generation rule.
- Boundary-condition preservation.
- Solver tolerances.
- Quantity-of-interest definitions.

Evaluate suitable quantities, including:

- Peak and representative temperature.
- Total electrical power.
- Integrated heat generation.
- Representative displacement or stress.
- Plastic strain where applicable.

Do not describe a mesh study as converged merely because the last two values are closer than the first two.

Investigate non-monotonic behavior, singular stress concentrations, under-resolved gradients, and boundary-condition inconsistencies.

Where applicable, calculate relative changes and estimate discretization error using a method whose assumptions are satisfied.

## D2. Load-step and coupling-step sensitivity

Compare results under different load increments or coupling tolerances.

Demonstrate that the reported engineering quantities are not artifacts of a single chosen step size or relaxation factor.

## D3. Numerical reproducibility

Repeat deterministic benchmark cases and record whether differences are within declared tolerances.

If nondeterminism is expected from parallel execution or solver ordering, document its source and establish a suitable reproducibility envelope.

